              "journal-title": "Amer. J. Bot."
            },
            {
              "key": "36_CR34",
              "doi-asserted-by": "crossref",
              "first-page": "557",
              "DOI": "10.1093/oxfordjournals.aob.a085767",
              "volume": "44",
              "author": "DA Charles-Edwards",
              "year": "1979",
              "unstructured": "Charles-Edwards, D.A. Cookshull, K.E. Horridge, J.S. and Thornley (1979) Ann. Bot. 44, 557\u2013566.",
              "journal-title": "Ann. Bot."
            },
            {
              "key": "36_CR35",
              "unstructured": "Which in reality would have been advected on the approximately cylindrical stem."
            },
            {
              "key": "36_CR36",
              "unstructured": "They also share the possibility of an abnormal apical meristem evolution, called the fasciation process: the apex becomes a long macroscopic line, instead of a staying a microscopic point. See for instance celosia cristata (the cockscomb) or some cacti. In this case of course the usual arrengements are not observed, and the growth forms a flattened shoot undergoing successive buckling instabilities."
            }
